// qoder_cipher.nv — AES-128-CBC for the Qoder credential store (task 035).
//
// Source: qodercli's own WASM module (`qoder_auth_wasm`,
// `credential_storage_encrypt/decrypt`), measured on synthetic inputs 2026-11-12; the
// reference's twin lives in `scripts/claude_limits.py` (`qoder_decrypt`/`qoder_encrypt`),
// and the scheme is written up in the task's `research.md`. The store (`~/.qoder/.auth/
// user`) is ONE line of text: base64 (STANDARD alphabet) of AES-128-CBC-PKCS7 over the
// login JSON, the key being the first 16 CHARACTERS of `.auth/machine_id` as ascii
// bytes -- and the IV is that same key. Nothing random in it: two encryptions of the
// same text give the same blob, which is why the committed fixture
// `fixtures/usage/qoder-blob.json` (built by the WASM itself) pins both implementations
// byte for byte.
//
// WHY BY HAND, again: the stdlib has no AES, the project admits no dependencies, and
// the owner chose (2026-10-31, for ZCode's GCM) a plain implementation over a new C-FFI.
// Unlike GCM, CBC DECRYPTS with the inverse cipher, so this file carries both
// directions, the S-box's inverse included. Vectors: FIPS-197 AES-128 and NIST SP
// 800-38A F.2.1 (CBC) -- here and in `scripts/test_qoder_creds.py`.
//
// PURE. The key arrives as the STRING `qoder_key` cuts -- the callers hold machine ids,
// not byte arrays. Bytes inside are `[]int` (0..255), as in `zcode_cipher.nv`.
//
// NO TOKEN LEAVES THIS FILE: the caller decides what a decrypted store means; this file
// turns bytes into bytes and never prints them.

module usage.qoder_cipher

import std.encoding.base64.{Base64}

/// Why a store did not open. Padding that does not check is the wrong-machine case
/// (the WASM says "Invalid PKCS5 padding"); shape is everything before that -- not
/// base64, a short store, a short key.
export type QoderCipherFail enum
    | Shape
    | Padding

export fn QoderCipherFail @to_str() -> str =>
    match @ {
        Shape => "malformed input"
        Padding => "invalid padding"
    }

// FIPS-197 §3.2.1 -- the SAME table `zcode_cipher.nv` carries, a literal on purpose for
// the same reason it gives there; its inverse below is derived from it, in Python, once
// (S and S⁻¹ are mutual permutations; the tests check the round trip anyway).
